  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nWhat has been generally zonal flow aloft will become increasingly \nmeridional over the coming days, leading to a more active period for \nweather. A cold front has slipped southward throughout the morning. \nLatest surface observations indicate this boundary now sits near the \nMissouri River with temperatures roughly 3-5 degF cooler on the \nnorth side of front. As for high temperatures today, we could see \nabout a 10 degF temperature difference between our northern and \nsouthern tiers of counties. The front is expected to continue \nsinking south throughout the afternoon and evening, becoming more \ndiffuse in the process. Isolated shower or storm development south \nof the boundary this afternoon remains possible under WAA \nconditions, but it seems such development would be quite the uphill \nbattle given poor shear and lackluster lapse rates. The arrival of \nthe cold front could inspire a brief shower or storm. Even so, I \nwould not expect anything remotely robust given the lack of upper-\nlevel support. Shower and storm activity appears to be more likely \n(40-70%) across northeastern Kansas and northern Missouri beginning \nlater this evening and then continuing through the early portions of \nthe day on Friday. Continued low-level WAA will promote a region of \nelevated instability (as much as 800 J/kg) in these areas by late \nevening. Lift looks to be provided by an approaching shortwave with \nseveral members of the guidance resolving a localized vort max \nnorth of the Missouri River around 06Z; roughly around this time\nis when CAMs also begin to resolve scattered showers and \nstorms. CAMs also present a backbuilding scenario across far \nnortheast Kansas into northwest Missouri...with PWATs between \n1.25-1.5\", isolated flooding may occur where showers/storms \ncontinuously move over the same area.\n\nScattered showers and storms will continue across portions of \nnorthern Kansas and Missouri for much of the day on Friday. Further \nsouth, the cold front currently crawling through our CWA will lift \nback north as a warm front in the afternoon/evening. This with an \napproaching mid-level trough will reinvigorate rain chances for much \nof the region Friday PM into Saturday AM (hourly PoPs about 40-70%). \nLow-end rain chances will continue for the remainder of the day as \nthe mid-level trough continues its journey through the \nPlains/Midwest. Generally along and west of the I-35 and I-49 \ncorridors, these chances are closer to 20-30% given a low-level dry \nlayer and little upper-level support from the vacating mid-level \ntrough. Further into central Missouri, these rain chances are \nslightly higher at 30-50% where the mid-level trough may still \nretain some influence.\n\nSunday continues to look dry with temperatures in the upper 60s/low \n70s. A deepening upper trough over the western CONUS will slide to \neastward over the weekend with its accompanying surface cyclone \nmoving into the Great Plains on Monday. This system will result in a \nmore active weather pattern than what we have become accustomed to \nin recent days and even some weeks. Indeed, the first half of the \nweek looks rather soggy (Monday and Tuesday especially). \nUnsurprisingly, the entire CWA as well as a large chunk of the \nCentral Plains are included in a Day 5 Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) \nfor excessive rainfall on Monday, Nov. 2. We will need to monitor \npotential instability corridors ahead of the  surface \nlow's attendant cold front as these areas would have an inherently \nhigher risk for flooding due to convectively-driven high rainfall \nrates.",
